{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfiles = [\n'/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed402918419/7/1.py',\n'/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed402918419/7/2.py',\n'/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed402918419/7/3.py',\n'/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed402918419/7/4.py',\n'/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed402918419/7/5.py',\n]\nfor path in files:\n    text = Path(path).read_text().splitlines()\n    print(f'FILE: {path}')\n    for name in ('main','generate_input'):\n        for i,l in enumerate(text):\n            if l.startswith(f'def {name}('):\n                end = min(len(text), i+80)\n                print(f'--- {name} ---')\n                print('\\\\n'.join(text[i:end]))\n                break\n    print('=== END FILE ===')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"FILE: /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed402918419/7/1.py\n--- main ---\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Project each framed interior motif outward in the direction marked by the frame gap.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - A dominant black background contains several one-pixel-thick rectangular frames, each missing exactly one border pixel on one side.\n    - Every frame is monochrome and encloses one or more same-colored interior blocks in another color.\n    - The missing border pixel indicates one of the four cardinal projection directions.\n\n    Rule:\n    - For each frame, detect which side contains the single-pixel gap.\n    - Count the number N of 4-connected interior blocks inside that frame.\n    - Treat all non-frame colored cells inside the frame as one motif and copy that motif N times outside the frame along the gap direction.\n    - Consecutive copies are separated by the full frame height for top/bottom gaps or the full frame width for left/right gaps, and the original frame stays unchanged.\n\n    Key insight:\n    - The gap chooses the projection axis, while the number of internal blocks chooses how many equally spaced copies to emit.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    assert g.ndim == 2\n\n    bg = _dominant_color(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n\n    for frame in _find_frames(g, bg):\n        _project_pattern(\n            out,\n            frame[\"motif_pixels\"],\n            frame[\"side\"],\n            int(frame[\"step\"]),\n            int(frame[\"repeats\"]),\n        )\n\n    return out\n\n\ndef _chain_bbox(\n    r0: int,\n    c0: int,\n    height: int,\n    width: int,\n    side: str,\n    repeats: int,\n) -> tuple[int, int, int, int]:\n    if side == \"top\":\n        return (r0 - repeats * height, c0, r0 + height - 1, c0 + width - 1)\n    if side == \"bottom\":\n        return (r0, c0, r0 + (repeats + 1) * height - 1, c0 + width - 1)\n    if side == \"left\":\n        return (r0, c0 - repeats * width, r0 + height - 1, c0 + width - 1)\n    return (r0, c0, r0 + height - 1, c0 + (repeats + 1) * width - 1)\n\n\ndef _bbox_is_free(blocked: np.ndarray, bbox: tuple[int, int, int, int]) -> bool:\n    r0, c0, r1, c1 = bbox\n    return not bool(blocked[r0 : r1 + 1, c0 : c1 + 1].any())\n\n\ndef _mark_blocked(blocked: np.ndarray, bbox: tuple[int, int, int, int], pad: int) -> None:\n    h, w = blocked.shape\n    r0, c0, r1, c1 = bbox\n    rr0 = max(0, r0 - pad)\n    cc0 = max(0, c0 - pad)\n    rr1 = min(h - 1, r1 + pad)\n    cc1 = min(w - 1, c1 + pad)\n    blocked[rr0 : rr1 + 1, cc0 : cc1 + 1] = True\n\n\ndef _paint_frame(\n    grid: np.ndarray,\n    r0: int,\n    c0: int,\n    height: int,\n    width: int,\n    color: int,\n    side: str,\n    gap_offset: int,\n    bg: int,\n) -> None:\n    grid[r0, c0 : c0 + width] = color\n--- generate_input ---\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n    for _ in range(GEN_ATTEMPTS):\n        grid = _gen_attempt()\n        if grid is not None:\n            return grid\n    raise RuntimeError(f\"failed to generate a valid input after {GEN_ATTEMPTS} attempts\")\n=== END FILE ===\nFILE: /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed402918419/7/2.py\n--- main ---\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Reassemble keyed line fragments back into their matching broken rectangle borders.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - Black background with several single-cell-thick rectangular borders in different colors.\n    - Each border color forms an incomplete rectangle: one or more straight edge segments are missing.\n    - Outside the frames are line fragments in other colors, and each fragment color appears once more as a single marker pixel inside exactly one frame.\n\n    Rule:\n    - Identify each frame by its incomplete rectangular border geometry.\n    - Use the interior marker pixel to pair one fragment color with one frame, then compare the outside fragment shapes with the missing perimeter segments of that frame.\n    - Remove the marker and the outside fragments, and fill the missing perimeter cells with the frame's border color.\n\n    Key insight:\n    - The marker pixel is the ownership key, while the fragment geometry reveals which border gaps must be restored.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    bg = _bg_color(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n\n    colors = [int(color) for color in np.unique(g) if int(color) != int(bg)]\n    frames: list[dict[str, object]] = []\n    frame_by_color: dict[int, int] = {}\n    for color in colors:\n        bbox = _frame_bbox_if_valid(g, int(color))\n        if bbox is None:\n            continue\n        frame_by_color[int(color)] = len(frames)\n        frames.append({\"frame_color\": int(color), \"bbox\": bbox})\n\n    assert frames, \"expected at least one incomplete frame\"\n\n    owner_by_piece_color: dict[int, int] = {}\n    marker_by_piece_color: dict[int, Coord] = {}\n    fragments_by_piece_color: dict[int, list[list[Coord]]] = {}\n\n    for color in colors:\n        if color in frame_by_color:\n            continue\n        coords = [tuple(map(int, coord)) for coord in np.argwhere(g == int(color))]\n        owner: int | None = None\n        marker: Coord | None = None\n        outside_mask = np.zeros(g.shape, dtype=bool)\n\n        for r, c in coords:\n            inside = []\n            for idx, frame in enumerate(frames):\n                r0, c0, r1, c1 = frame[\"bbox\"]  # type: ignore[index]\n                if int(r0) < r < int(r1) and int(c0) < c < int(c1):\n                    inside.append(idx)\n            if inside:\n                assert len(inside) == 1, \"marker cannot belong to multiple frames\"\n                if owner is None:\n                    owner = int(inside[0])\n                assert owner == int(inside[0]), \"a fragment color must map to one frame\"\n                assert marker is None, \"expected exactly one marker pixel\"\n                marker = (int(r), int(c))\n            else:\n                outside_mask[int(r), int(c)] = True\n\n        assert owner is not None and marker is not None, \"piece color must have one interior marker\"\n        fragment_comps = _components(outside_mask)\n        assert fragment_comps, \"piece color must have outside fragments\"\n        owner_by_piece_color[int(color)] = int(owner)\n        marker_by_piece_color[int(color)] = marker\n        fragments_by_piece_color[int(color)] = fragment_comps\n\n    piece_color_for_frame: dict[int, int] = {}\n    for piece_color, owner in owner_by_piece_color.items():\n        assert owner not in piece_color_for_frame, \"each frame must have a unique marker color\"\n        piece_color_for_frame[int(owner)] = int(piece_color)\n\n    for idx, frame in enumerate(frames):\n        assert idx in piece_color_for_frame, \"every frame must have a marker/piece color\"\n        piece_color = int(piece_color_for_frame[idx])\n        frame_color = int(frame[\"frame_color\"])  # type: ignore[index]\n        r0, c0, r1, c1 = frame[\"bbox\"]  # type: ignore[index]\n\n        border = _border_coords(int(r0), int(c0), int(r1), int(c1))\n--- generate_input ---\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n    for _ in range(GEN_ATTEMPTS):\n        grid = _gen_attempt()\n        if grid is not None:\n            return grid\n    raise RuntimeError(\"failed to generate a valid input\")\n=== END FILE ===\nFILE: /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed402918419/7/3.py\n--- main ---\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"Map each object's hole count through the legend, then redraw the objects as nested centered rectangles ordered by area.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - A full gray divider column splits the grid into a left object field and a right legend field.\n    - The left field has black background and several separated monochrome 4-connected foreground objects.\n    - Objects may contain zero or more fully enclosed black single-pixel holes.\n    - The right field is a hole-count legend: in each used row, red dots encode the count and one colored cell gives the output color.\n\n    Rule:\n    - Read the right legend as a mapping from red-dot count to target color.\n    - For every left-side object, count its enclosed black hole pixels and look up the corresponding target color.\n    - Sort the objects by foreground area from large to small.\n    - Return only the right-field canvas size, drawing centered filled rectangles in that order so each rectangle area equals the source object area and uses the mapped color.\n\n    Key insight:\n    - The source object color is irrelevant: the visible red-dot legend converts hole count into the repaint color, and the actual redraw geometry depends only on area order.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    assert g.ndim == 2\n\n    divider_cols = [c for c in range(g.shape[1]) if np.all(g[:, c] == DIVIDER)]\n    assert len(divider_cols) == 1, \"expected one full gray divider column\"\n    divider = divider_cols[0]\n\n    left = g[:, :divider]\n    right = g[:, divider + 1 :]\n    assert left.size > 0 and right.size > 0\n\n    legend = _parse_legend(right)\n    objects = _same_color_components(left)\n    assert objects, \"expected foreground objects in the left field\"\n\n    specs = []\n    for obj in objects:\n        holes = _count_object_holes(left, obj)\n        assert holes in legend, \"every hole count must appear in the legend\"\n        specs.append((int(obj[\"area\"]), int(legend[holes])))\n\n    out_h, out_w = right.shape\n    out = np.full((out_h, out_w), BG, dtype=int)\n    for area, color in sorted(specs, key=lambda item: item[0], reverse=True):\n        rect_h, rect_w = _best_rect_dims(area)\n        assert rect_h <= out_h and rect_w <= out_w, \"rectangle must fit in the legend-sized output\"\n        r0 = (out_h - rect_h) // 2\n        c0 = (out_w - rect_w) // 2\n        out[r0 : r0 + rect_h, c0 : c0 + rect_w] = color\n    return out\n\n\ndef _random_transform(mask: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    out = mask.copy()\n    if random.random() < 0.5:\n        out = np.flipud(out)\n    if random.random() < 0.5:\n        out = np.fliplr(out)\n    if random.random() < 0.5:\n        out = out.T\n    return out.copy()\n\n\ndef _frontier(mask: np.ndarray) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\n    h, w = mask.shape\n    cells: set[tuple[int, int]] = set()\n    for r, c in zip(*np.where(mask), strict=False):\n        for nr, nc in _neighbors4(int(r), int(c)):\n            if 0 <= nr < h and 0 <= nc < w and not mask[nr, nc]:\n                cells.add((nr, nc))\n    return list(cells)\n\n\ndef _initial_mask(holes: int) -> np.ndarray:\n    if holes == 0:\n        seeds = [\n            np.array([[1, 1], [1, 0]], dtype=bool),\n            np.array([[1, 1, 0], [0, 1, 1]], dtype=bool),\n            np.array([[1, 1, 1], [0, 1, 0]], dtype=bool),\n        ]\n        return random.choice(seeds).copy()\n\n--- generate_input ---\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n    for _ in range(GEN_RETRIES):\n        grid = _gen_attempt()\n        if grid is not None:\n            return grid\n    raise RuntimeError(\"failed to generate a valid hole-legend ARC input\")\n=== END FILE ===\nFILE: /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed402918419/7/4.py\n--- main ---\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Infer the crossing stack order of colored border-to-border paths and keep only the unique topmost path.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - The background is black.\n    - Each non-black color denotes one single-pixel-wide 4-connected path whose two endpoints lie on the grid border.\n    - Paths may cross orthogonally, and at each crossing only the upper path's color is visible at the shared cell.\n\n    Rule:\n    - Reconstruct each path through hidden crossings by detecting cells where the same color continues straight on opposite sides of another visible color.\n    - Every reconstructed hidden crossing gives a layering relation: the reconstructed color is below the visible color at that cell.\n    - Find the unique path color that is never below any other path, then output only that path on a black canvas.\n\n    Key insight:\n    The geometry of a hidden lower path is recoverable locally from straight opposite-side continuations, and those local recoveries are enough to identify the one path that is never covered.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    assert g.ndim == 2\n\n    colors = [int(color) for color in np.unique(g) if int(color) != BG]\n    assert colors, \"expected at least one path\"\n\n    if len(colors) == 1:\n        return g.copy()\n\n    reconstructed: dict[int, np.ndarray] = {}\n    hidden_under: dict[int, set[int]] = {}\n    for color in colors:\n        reconstructed[color], hidden_under[color] = _reconstruct_path_mask(g, color)\n\n    top_candidates = [color for color in colors if not hidden_under[color]]\n    assert len(top_candidates) == 1, \"expected a unique topmost path\"\n    top_color = top_candidates[0]\n\n    out = np.zeros_like(g)\n    out[reconstructed[top_color]] = top_color\n    return out\n\n\ndef _sample_spaced(\n    rng: np.random.Generator, candidates: list[int], count: int, min_gap: int = 2\n) -> list[int] | None:\n    if count == 0:\n        return []\n    if count > len(candidates):\n        return None\n\n    for _ in range(200):\n        chosen = sorted(int(v) for v in rng.choice(candidates, size=count, replace=False))\n        if all(b - a >= min_gap for a, b in zip(chosen, chosen[1:])):\n            return chosen\n    return None\n\n\ndef _render_paths(\n    shape: tuple[int, int], paths_in_z_order: list[dict[str, object]]\n) -> np.ndarray:\n    grid = np.zeros(shape, dtype=int)\n    for path in paths_in_z_order:\n        coords = np.asarray(path[\"points\"], dtype=int)\n        grid[coords[:, 0], coords[:, 1]] = int(path[\"color\"])\n    return grid\n\n\ndef _top_mask(shape: tuple[int, int], points: list[tuple[int, int]], color: int) -> np.ndarray:\n    mask = np.zeros(shape, dtype=int)\n    coords = np.asarray(points, dtype=int)\n    mask[coords[:, 0], coords[:, 1]] = color\n    return mask\n\n\ndef _gen_straight_horizontal(\n    rng: np.random.Generator,\n) -> tuple[tuple[int, int], list[list[tuple[int, int]]], list[tuple[int, int]]] | None:\n    h = int(rng.integers(12, 25))\n    w = int(rng.integers(12, 25))\n    top_row = int(rng.integers(3, h - 3))\n\n    candidates = list(range(2, w - 2))\n--- generate_input ---\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n    rng = np.random.default_rng()\n    for _ in range(MAX_GEN_ATTEMPTS):\n        grid = _generate_candidate(rng)\n        if grid is not None:\n            return grid\n    raise RuntimeError(\"failed to generate a valid path-stacking ARC input\")\n=== END FILE ===\nFILE: /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed402918419/7/5.py\n--- main ---\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Extend each seed outward along the hidden rail implied by its blue anchor pair.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - The background is black.\n    - One or more gray one-pixel-thick rectangular fences appear on the grid.\n    - Each fence contains exactly one non-black, non-gray seed pixel.\n    - Outside the fences, blue pixels appear in disjoint pairs; each pair lies on one\n      horizontal, vertical, or 45-degree diagonal rail line.\n\n    Rule:\n    - For each gray fence, find its unique interior seed and compute the fence perimeter\n      as the number of gray border pixels.\n    - Find the unique blue-anchor rail whose line is collinear with that seed.\n    - Starting from the seed, walk along the rail in both directions for exactly that\n      many steps, stopping early at the grid edge.\n    - Recolor only black cells on that walked path with the seed color; keep every fence\n      and every fenced interior cell unchanged.\n\n    Key insight:\n    - The blue anchor pairs define invisible rail directions, while the gray fence size\n      supplies the propagation length through its perimeter.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    out = g.copy()\n    frames = _find_frames(g)\n    anchors = [tuple(map(int, pt)) for pt in np.argwhere(g == ANCHOR)]\n    rail_keys = _rail_keys_from_anchors(anchors)\n    protected = set()\n    for frame in frames:\n        protected.update(_bbox_cells(*frame[\"bbox\"]))\n\n    for frame in frames:\n        seed = frame[\"seed\"]\n        matching = [key for key in rail_keys if _point_on_line(seed, key)]\n        assert len(matching) == 1, \"each seed must align with exactly one rail\"\n        step = _step_for_key(matching[0])\n        for sign in (-1, 1):\n            for dist in range(1, frame[\"perimeter\"] + 1):\n                r = seed[0] + sign * step[0] * dist\n                c = seed[1] + sign * step[1] * dist\n                if not _in_bounds(g.shape, r, c):\n                    break\n                if (r, c) in protected:\n                    continue\n                if out[r, c] == BG:\n                    out[r, c] = frame[\"seed_color\"]\n    return out\n\n\ndef generate_